				</button>
				<text_box vertical_text_align="center" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" x="90" y="180" width="540" height="301">A Confidence Graph shows how a variable in a system changes over time across multiple simulation runs or scenarios. It is used to communicate the uncertainty or variability in model outcomes. This type of graph helps us understand not only what might happen, but also how confident we are in those predictions—based on differences in the assumptions or input values used in the simulations.

It typically includes:

- A central line showing the average or median behavior of the variable

- Shaded bands or lines above and below that represent the range or     confidence interval (e.g., 5th to 95th percentile)

- A time axis (horizontal) and a value axis (vertical)
</text_box>
				<isee:shape z_index="15" border_color="#989898" border_width="0.5" opacity="1" transparent_background="false" x="683.15" y="95" width="14" height="454" kind="vline"/>
				<text_box text_align="center" vertical_text_align="center" font_size="20pt" appearance="transparent" x="20" y="101.5" width="668" height="58">Confidence Graph</text_box>
				<graphics_frame z_index="16" fill="none" x="724.51" y="180.5" width="300" height="300">
					<image width="585" height="427" size_to_parent="true" isee:transparency="0" isee:fixed_aspect_ratio="true">r../Graphics/Confidence Graph.png</image>
				</graphics_frame>
				<isee:shape background="white" border_width="0.5" border_style="none" transparent_background="false" corner_radius="15" x="20" y="94" width="1026" height="456" isee:locked="true" kind="rectangle"/>
				<isee:navigation_widget border_width="thin" border_style="none" item_spacing="1" orientation="horizontal" x="50.4136" y="64" width="884.545" height="30">
