				<isee:annotation z_index="80" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="30" y="106.83" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="349.278" y="58.389" width="423.444" height="269.111">
						<text>A carbon tax is a fee paid to governments by individuals and businesses for each unit of greenhouse gas emitted from fossil fuels like coal, oil, and gas.

Some policymakers suggest setting the tax equal to the &quot;Social Cost of Carbon,&quot; an estimate of climate damage per ton of emissions. Estimates vary widely: $43 (Obama), $51 (Biden), $3–$5 (Trump), and $190 (EPA, 2022), reflecting differing views on its appropriate level.

Proponents argue a carbon tax incentivizes cleaner energy use and reduces global warming while generating government revenue. Critics claim it could slow economic growth by raising prices on goods and services.

The speed of implementation is also debated, supporters favor quick action to cut emissions, while opponents warn it could cause economic disruption if done too rapidly.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="73" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="31" y="314.83" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="350.278" y="275" width="393.444" height="237.5">
						<text>A nuclear energy tax/subsidy is a government tax, or incentive (tax breaks or grants) for building nuclear power plants. 

Supporters say nuclear offers reliable, low-carbon energy crucial for climate goals. The U.S. Inflation Reduction Act includes production tax credits for existing and new plants.

Critics point to high costs, long construction times, and radioactive waste risks. Some argue funds would be better spent on renewables and storage.

Debates center on safety, cost-effectiveness, and nuclear’s role in a clean energy mix.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="82" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="374" y="108.83" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="6.278" y="106" width="346.444" height="223">
						<text>A hydropower energy tax/subsidy is a government tax, or incentive (tax breaks or grants), given to penalize or support energy generated from water sources.

Supporters say it boosts clean energy, cuts fossil fuel use, and helps grid stability.

Critics argue it can harm ecosystems, displace communities, and favor an already mature technology over emerging ones like solar.

Debate continues over how much to subsidize and how fast to expand support.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="84" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="718.06" y="106.83" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="308.278" y="102" width="367.444" height="223">
						<text>A bioenergy tax/subsidy is a government tax, or incentive (tax breaks or grants) for energy made from organic materials like crops, wood, or waste.

Supporters argue it reduces fossil fuel use, supports rural economies, and uses existing waste streams. The U.S. and EU offer billions in biofuel and biomass incentives presently.

Critics say bioenergy can drive deforestation, compete with food crops, and may not cut emissions as much as claimed.

Debates focus on sustainability, land use impacts, and whether subsidies should shift to cleaner alternatives.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="75" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="374" y="315" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="17.556" y="274.5" width="330.888" height="238.5">
						<text>A solar energy tax/subsidy is government tax, or incentive (tax breaks or grants) for installing or producing solar power. 

Supporters say it cuts emissions, lowers energy bills, and accelerates the shift to clean energy. The U.S. offers a 30% federal tax credit for residential and commercial solar.

Critics argue subsidies can burden taxpayers, strain grids at high penetration, or favor wealthier households with rooftop access.

Debates focus on grid integration, equity, and how long support should continue as costs fall.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="78" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="718.06" y="315" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="307.278" y="289" width="367.444" height="223">
						<text>A wind energy tax/subsidy is government tax, or incentive (tax breaks or grants) for installing or producing solar power. 

Backers cite clean energy, declining costs, and rural job growth. In the U.S., the Investment Tax Credit (ITC) can cover up to 30% of project costs for wind (2023).

Critics note land use conflicts, wildlife impacts, and power variability. Some argue funds should go to grid improvements or storage instead.

Debates focus on cost-effectiveness, community acceptance, and integration into the broader energy mix.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
