				<slider z_index="99" color="#0075FF" font_size="11pt" num_ticks="2" label_side="top" isee:show_hover_tip="when_deciding" input_expands="false" input_width="200" x="60" y="355" width="260" height="40" isee:locked="true" title="Central Bank Inflation Target" isee:navigate_to="false" show_name="true">
					<entity name="Government_Regulations.inflation_goal_policy"/>
					<reset_to after="never">0</reset_to>
				</slider>
				<isee:annotation z_index="88" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="31" y="109" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="349.278" y="88" width="423.444" height="222.111">
						<text>A minimum percentage of taxes spent policy requires governments to allocate a set portion of tax revenues to be spent.  Low values allow governments to easily manage their debts but potentially reduce services for citizens.  High values do the opposite.

Supporters of a high minimum percentage say it ensures consistent funding for important priorities and accountability in public spending.

Critics of a high minimum percentage argue it limits budget flexibility and may not match changing needs or emergencies.

Debates revolve around the right percentage, target areas, and balancing fiscal responsibility with policy goals.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="87" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="373.605" y="107.318" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="6.278" y="106" width="346.444" height="223">
						<text>The debt-to-GDP ratio is a measure comparing the world&apos;s total public debt to its economic output (GDP), showing how much debt exists relative to the economy’s size.

Policymakers use it to assess fiscal health, lower ratios suggest more sustainable debt levels.

Critics warn high ratios can limit government spending, increase borrowing costs, and risk financial instability.

Debates focus on acceptable thresholds, impacts on growth, and how to balance debt with investment needs.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="74" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="716.787" y="107.5" width="20.426" height="17.6364" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="308.278" y="102" width="367.444" height="223">
						<text>A wage tax is a government tax on employees’ earnings, usually deducted directly from paychecks.

Supporters say it funds public services like healthcare and education and can be structured progressively to reduce inequality.

Critics argue high wage taxes can discourage work, reduce take-home pay, and increase labor costs for employers.

Debates focus on tax rates, fairness, and balancing revenue with economic incentives.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="90" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="29.6957" y="316" width="22.6086" height="18" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="350.278" y="134" width="393.444" height="378.5">
						<text>Central bank inflation target is a goal set by a country’s central bank to keep inflation around a specific rate, often near 2%.

Supporters say it helps maintain price stability, supports economic growth, and anchors expectations.

Critics argue rigid targets can limit policy flexibility, especially during shocks, and may overlook other economic goals.

Debates focus on the ideal inflation rate and how strictly to enforce targets.

*******************************************

Central bank unemployment target is a goal to achieve a low or “natural” rate of unemployment through monetary policy.

Supporters claim it promotes maximum employment and supports economic well-being.

Critics caution that focusing too much on unemployment may fuel inflation or create asset bubbles.

Debates center on balancing unemployment with inflation control and the limits of monetary policy.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="90" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="373" y="313.273" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="17.556" y="323" width="330.888" height="190">
						<text>A profit tax is a government levy on the net earnings of businesses.

Supporters say it generates revenue for public services and can promote fair wealth distribution.

Critics argue high rates may discourage investment, reduce competitiveness, and encourage tax avoidance.

Debates center on setting rates that balance revenue needs with economic growth incentives.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:annotation z_index="90" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="716.787" y="313.83" width="20" height="20" isee:locked="true">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="307.278" y="289" width="367.444" height="223">
						<text>A wealth tax is a government charge on the total value of an individual’s assets, like property, investments, and savings.

Supporters say it reduces inequality and raises revenue from the richest individuals.

Critics argue it’s hard to value assets accurately, may drive capital flight, and could discourage investment.

Debates focus on tax thresholds, rates, enforcement challenges, and economic impacts.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<isee:shape z_index="94" background="#204575" border_width="thin" border_style="none" transparent_background="false" x="319.364" y="109.352" width="29" height="23.2964" isee:locked="true" block_mouse="true" kind="rectangle"/>
			</view>
			<view isee:template_view="template:25" isee:name="Dashboard Inputs - SLR" background="#082A5C" page_width="1066" page_height="600" zoom="180" type="interface">
				<style/>
				<options z_index="97" appearance="button-bar" arrangement="vertical" x="399.993" y="357.461" width="271.381" height="100">
					<entity name="Government_Regulations.No_SLR_adaptation_strategy" title="Do nothing">
						<reset_to after="never">0</reset_to>
					</entity>
					<entity name="Government_Regulations.Protection_as_SLR_adaptation_strategy" title="Build sea walls">
						<reset_to after="never">0</reset_to>
					</entity>
					<entity name="Government_Regulations.Retreat_as_SLR_adaptation_strategy" title="Retreat from the coast">
						<reset_to after="never">0</reset_to>
					</entity>
				</options>
				<text_box z_index="97" text_align="center" vertical_text_align="center" appearance="transparent" x="399.993" y="309.279" width="271.381" height="42.7273">How does government respond to Sea Level Rise?</text_box>
				<slider z_index="98" color="#0075FF" num_ticks="4" label_side="top" isee:show_hover_tip="when_deciding" input_expands="false" input_width="250" x="403.001" y="185.04" width="260" height="40" increment="5" title="Time to Start SLR Policy" isee:navigate_to="false" show_name="true">
					<entity name="Government_Regulations.SLR_adaptation_start_year"/>
					<reset_to after="never">0</reset_to>
				</slider>
				<graphics_frame z_index="34" fill="none" x="377.993" y="112.348" width="22" height="22">
					<image width="256" height="256" size_to_parent="true" isee:transparency="0" isee:fixed_aspect_ratio="true">r../Graphics/question mark icon white.png</image>
				</graphics_frame>
				<isee:annotation z_index="36" transparent="true" launcher_label="" show_by_default="false" hide_launcher="false" launch_on_hover="true" x="222" y="180.929" width="22.1739" height="19.5652">
					<popup hide_when_editing="true" x="90.278" y="84.0595" width="423.444" height="468.701">
						<text>Do nothing means accepting flooding and damage without intervention.

Supporters say it avoids high costs and lets nature adapt naturally.

Critics warn it risks property loss, ecosystem damage, and community displacement.

Debates focus on risk tolerance and economic trade-offs.

****

Build sea walls involves constructing barriers to block rising waters.

Proponents highlight protection of homes, infrastructure, and economic assets.

Opponents point to high costs, environmental impacts, and limited long-term effectiveness.

Debates center on cost-benefit and sustainability.

*****

Retreat from the coast means relocating people and infrastructure inland.

Supporters say it’s a long-term, sustainable solution to avoid repeated damage.

Critics argue it’s socially disruptive, expensive, and politically difficult.

Debates focus on timing, funding, and community consent.</text>
					</popup>
				</isee:annotation>
				<graphics_frame fill="none" x="370" y="104" width="328" height="390">
					<image width="1118" height="1339" size_to_parent="true" isee:transparency="0" isee:fixed_aspect_ratio="true">r../Graphics/Dashboard Input Panel 3 Holders.png</image>
				</graphics_frame>
