	<model name="CLD_Policy_1">
		<variables>
			<aux name="Surface\nTemperature\nAnomaly" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Economic_Output,Speed_of_Energy_Transition)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Economic\nOutput" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature_Anomaly,Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets,Government_Revenue,Employment,Investment,Inflation)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Carbon Tax" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN</eqn>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Government Revenue" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Carbon_Tax,Employment,Economic_Output)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Bankruptcies\nStranded Assets" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Speed_of_Energy_Transition,&quot;Solar-Wind-Nuclear_Subsidies&quot;)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Employment" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets,Investment)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Investment" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets,Economic_Output,Bank_Bailouts)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Inflation" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Investment_Growth_Relative_to_Overall_Economic_Growth)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Investment Growth\nRelative to Overall\nEconomic Growth" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Investment,Economic_Output)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Speed of\nEnergy Transition" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Carbon_Tax,&quot;Solar-Wind-Nuclear_Subsidies&quot;)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Bank Bailouts" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN(Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Solar-Wind-Nuclear\nSubsidies" ai_state="B">
				<eqn>NAN</eqn>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
		</variables>
		<views>
			<view isee:show_pages="false" background="#FBF5F0" page_width="1063" page_height="771" isee:page_cols="2" isee:page_rows="2" zoom="80" isee:popup_graphs_are_comparative="true" isee:enable_non_negative_highlights="false" type="stock_flow">
				<style/>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="292.19" y="432.667" name="Surface\nTemperature\nAnomaly">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" background="white" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="479.107" y="271.286" name="Economic\nOutput">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" background="white" font_weight="bold" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="16pt" label_side="left" x="217.732" y="662.161" name="Carbon Tax">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="1" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="359.858">
					<isee:documentation>Carbon tax ↑ → Speed of energy transition ↑
When the carbon tax increases (↑), fossil energy becomes more expensive. This pushes firms and households to shift toward cleaner technologies, speeding up the energy transition (↑).

Carbon tax ↓ → Speed of energy transition ↓
When the carbon tax is lower (↓), fossil fuels remain cheaper. There is less incentive to invest in renewables, slowing down the speed of the energy transition (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Carbon tax → Speed of energy transition</isee:label>
					<from>Carbon_Tax</from>
					<to>Speed_of_Energy_Transition</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="2" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="28.8585">
					<isee:documentation>Surface Temperature Anomaly ↑ → Economic output ↓
When surface temperature anomaly increases (↑), the economy faces multiple climate related stresses such as heatwaves, reduced labor productivity, crop losses, infrastructure damage, and higher health costs. These impacts slow economic activity and reduce overall production, causing economic output to decrease (↓).

Surface Temperature Anomaly ↓ → Economic output ↑
When surface temperature anomaly decreases (↓), climate related disruptions are smaller. Fewer heat related losses and more stable environmental conditions allow businesses, agriculture, and labor markets to function more efficiently, supporting higher economic output (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Temperature (STA) → Economic growth (GDP per capita)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="218.607" y="230.75" name="Government Revenue">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" background="white" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="934.071" y="271.286" name="Bankruptcies\nStranded Assets">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="6" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="140.708">
					<isee:documentation>Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↑ → Economic output ↓
When bankruptcies from stranded assets increase (↑), businesses lose value, financial institutions face losses, and investment confidence drops. This slows business activity and reduces production, causing economic output to decrease (↓).

Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↓ → Economic output ↑
When bankruptcies decline (↓), financial stability improves and firms remain productive. This supports higher investment and economic activity, increasing economic output (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Bankruptcies from stranded assets → Economic growth (GDP per capita)</isee:label>
					<from>Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="7" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="335.777">
					<isee:documentation>Government revenue ↑ → Economic output ↑
When government revenue increases (↑), more funds are available for public investment. This stimulates business activity, boosts productivity, and supports higher economic output (↑).

Government revenue ↓ → Economic output ↓
When government revenue decreases (↓), the state has fewer resources to invest in public services, infrastructure, innovation, and social programs. Reduced public investment weakens overall economic activity, leading to lower economic output (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Government revenue → Economic growth (GDP per capita)</isee:label>
					<from>Government_Revenue</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="567.107" y="98.3567" name="Employment">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="8" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="133.058">
					<isee:documentation>Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↑ → Employment ↓
When bankruptcies from stranded fossil-fuel assets increase (↑), firms close or downsize and supply chains are disrupted. As companies fail, jobs are lost and employment decreases (↓).

Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↓ → Employment ↑
When bankruptcies decline (↓), firms remain operational and financial conditions stabilize. Businesses retain or expand their workforce, increasing employment (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Bankruptcies from stranded assets → Employment </isee:label>
					<from>Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets</from>
					<to>Employment</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="9" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="182.173">
					<isee:documentation>Employment ↑ → Government revenue ↑
When employment increases (↑), more people earn wages and pay income taxes. Higher payroll and income-tax collections increase government revenue (↑).

Employment ↓ → Government revenue ↓
When employment decreases (↓), fewer people earn taxable income. This reduces tax receipts and lowers government revenue (↓).

</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Employment → Government Revenue</isee:label>
					<from>Employment</from>
					<to>Government_Revenue</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="10" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="217.993">
					<isee:documentation>Employment ↑ → Economic output ↑
When employment rises (↑), more people participate in production. Higher labor input increases total output, raising economic output (↑).

Employment ↓ → Economic output ↓
When employment falls (↓), fewer people produce goods and services. Lower output reduces economic output (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Employment → Economic growth (GDP per capita) </isee:label>
					<from>Employment</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="11" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="148.14">
					<isee:documentation>Economic output ↑ → Government revenue ↑
When economic output increases (↑), businesses earn more and workers receive higher incomes. This raises both profit taxes and wage taxes, resulting in higher overall government revenue (↑).

Economic output ↓ → Government revenue ↓
When economic output decreases (↓), incomes and profits fall. With less taxable activity, the government collects fewer taxes, reducing government revenue (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Economic growth (GDP per capita) → Government revenue</isee:label>
					<from>Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Government_Revenue</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="12" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="202.812">
					<isee:documentation>Economic output ↑ → Surface Temperature Anomaly ↑
When economic output increases (↑), economic activity expands with more production, more transportation, and higher energy use. If this growth relies on fossil fuels, CO₂ and other greenhouse gas emissions increase, leading to a rise in surface temperature anomaly (↑).

Economic output ↓ → Surface Temperature Anomaly ↓
When economic output decreases (↓), energy use and emissions fall. With fewer emissions entering the atmosphere, the pressure driving temperature increase weakens, reducing the rate of surface temperature anomaly (↓).
</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Economic growth (GDP per capita)  → Temperature (STA) </isee:label>
					<from>Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="683.607" y="360.25" name="Investment">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="14" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="131.811">
					<isee:documentation>Investment ↑ → Economic output ↑
When investment increases (↑), more capital is created, such as infrastructure, machinery, and technology. This boosts productivity and expands overall production, leading to higher economic output (↑).

Investment ↓ → Economic output ↓
When investment falls (↓), fewer productive assets are built, slowing improvements in capacity and productivity. As a result, economic output grows more slowly or may decline (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Investment → Economic growth (GDP per capita)</isee:label>
					<from>Investment</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="15" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="325.306">
					<isee:documentation>Economic output ↑ → Investment ↑
When economic output increases (↑), businesses earn more, households have higher incomes, and overall economic confidence rises. With more profits and savings available, both firms and consumers invest more (↑).

Economic output ↓ → Investment ↓
When economic output decreases (↓), profits shrink and uncertainty rises. Firms delay or cut back on new projects, and households reduce savings and spending, leading to lower investment (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Economic growth (GDP per capita) → Investment </isee:label>
					<from>Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Investment</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="518.274" y="418.833" name="Inflation">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="16" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="71.7946">
					<isee:documentation>Inflation ↑ → Economic output ↓
When inflation increases (↑), the cost of goods, services, wages, and inputs rises across the economy. Higher inflation reduces purchasing power, increases uncertainty for businesses, and discourages investment. As a result, economic output decreases (↓).

Inflation ↓ → Economic output ↑
When inflation falls (↓), costs stabilize and purchasing power improves. Businesses face less uncertainty and invest more confidently. This supports higher economic output (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Inflation → Economic growth (GDP per capita) </isee:label>
					<from>Inflation</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="542.607" y="555.048" name="Investment Growth\nRelative to Overall\nEconomic Growth">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="17" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="264.805">
					<isee:documentation>Investment ↑ → Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↑
When investment increases (↑), financial activity expands faster than the rest of the economy. This raises the ratio of investment growth compared to overall economic growth (↑).

Investment ↓ → Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↓
When investment decreases (↓), financial activity grows more slowly than the broader economy, reducing the relative investment growth rate (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Investment ↑ → Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↑</isee:label>
					<from>Investment</from>
					<to>Investment_Growth_Relative_to_Overall_Economic_Growth</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="18" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="69.5549">
					<isee:documentation>Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↑ → Inflation ↑
When investment grows faster than the overall economy (↑), demand for inputs, labor, and capital rises faster than supply can adjust, creating price pressures. This imbalance pushes inflation upward (↑).

Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↓ → Inflation ↓
When investment grows more slowly than the overall economy (↓), resource demand eases and supply keeps up more easily, reducing upward pressure on prices and lowering inflation (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↑ → Inflation ↑ </isee:label>
					<from>Investment_Growth_Relative_to_Overall_Economic_Growth</from>
					<to>Inflation</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="571.607" y="663.036" name="Speed of\nEnergy Transition">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="19" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="157.367">
					<isee:documentation>Speed of energy transition ↑ → Surface temperature (STA) ↓

When the speed of the energy transition increases (↑), societies shift more rapidly from fossil fuels to clean energy sources like solar, wind, and nuclear. This reduces CO₂ and methane emissions, slowing the accumulation of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere. As a result, surface temperature rises more slowly or even declines relative to the baseline (STA ↓).

Speed of energy transition ↓ → Surface temperature (STA) ↑

A slower energy transition (↓) means fossil fuel use remains high for longer, leading to higher greenhouse gas emissions. This accelerates warming, causing surface temperature to increase faster (STA ↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Speed of energy transition → Surface temperature (STA)</isee:label>
					<from>Speed_of_Energy_Transition</from>
					<to>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="20" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="30.395">
					<isee:documentation>Speed of energy transition ↑ → Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↑
A faster energy transition (↑) reduces the value of fossil-fuel assets more quickly. As these assets lose profitability, companies holding them face financial losses, leading to more bankruptcies from stranded assets (↑).

Speed of energy transition ↓ → Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↓
When the energy transition slows (↓), fossil-fuel assets decline more gradually, allowing companies more time to adjust. This results in fewer stranded assets and fewer bankruptcies (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Speed of energy transition → Bankruptcies from stranded assets</isee:label>
					<from>Speed_of_Energy_Transition</from>
					<to>Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="14pt" x="757.286" y="297.536" name="Bank Bailouts">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="21" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="165.753">
					<isee:documentation>Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↑ → Bank bailouts ↑
When bankruptcies from stranded fossil-fuel assets increase (↑), banks face rising losses and financial instability. To prevent systemic collapse, governments step in with bank bailouts, causing bailouts to increase (↑).

Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↓ → Bank bailouts ↓
When bankruptcies decline (↓), banks remain stable and require less emergency support, so bailouts decrease (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Bankruptcies from stranded assets → Bank bailouts </isee:label>
					<from>Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets</from>
					<to>Bank_Bailouts</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" background="white" font_weight="bold" font_color="#2E2E2E" font_size="16pt" label_side="right" x="934.071" y="663.036" name="Solar-Wind-Nuclear\nSubsidies">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="23" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="90">
					<isee:documentation>Solar–wind–nuclear subsidies ↑ → Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↓
When subsidies for solar, wind, and nuclear energy increase (↑), clean technologies become more competitive and scale up faster. This reduces reliance on fossil-fuel assets, slowing their decline and lowering the number of stranded fossil-fuel investments. As a result, bankruptcies from stranded assets decrease (↓).

Solar–wind–nuclear subsidies ↓ → Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↑
When renewable subsidies fall (↓), the shift away from fossil fuels slows. Fossil-fuel assets remain exposed to long-term transition risks, increasing the likelihood that they lose value and become stranded, which raises bankruptcies (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Solar–wind–nuclear subsidies → Bankruptcies from stranded assets</isee:label>
					<from>&quot;Solar-Wind-Nuclear_Subsidies&quot;</from>
					<to>Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="26" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="209.047">
					<isee:documentation>Bank bailouts ↑ → Investment ↑
When bank bailouts increase (↑), governments inject money to stabilize the financial sector. Banks recover liquidity and confidence, allowing them to increase lending and stimulate investment (↑).

Bank bailouts ↓ → Investment ↓
When bailouts decrease (↓), struggling banks remain constrained. With less liquidity and higher risk, they reduce lending, which lowers investment (↓).
</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Bank bailouts → Investment</isee:label>
					<from>Bank_Bailouts</from>
					<to>Investment</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="32" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="92.9252">
					<isee:documentation>Investment ↑ → Employment ↑
When investment increases (↑), businesses expand operations, launch new projects, and increase production capacity. This creates more job opportunities, causing employment to rise (↑).

Investment ↓ → Employment ↓
When investment falls (↓), firms delay expansion or cut projects. Hiring slows or layoffs occur, reducing employment (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Investment → Employment </isee:label>
					<from>Investment</from>
					<to>Employment</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="33" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="180">
					<isee:documentation>Solar–wind–nuclear subsidies ↑ → Speed of energy transition ↑
When subsidies for solar, wind, and nuclear energy increase (↑), clean technologies become cheaper and more competitive. This accelerates the shift toward low-carbon energy systems (↑).

Solar–wind–nuclear subsidies ↓ → Speed of energy transition ↓
When these subsidies are reduced (↓), renewable and nuclear options become relatively more expensive. This slows down the adoption of clean energy and reduces the speed of the transition (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Solar wind nuclear subsidies → Speed of energy transition</isee:label>
					<from>&quot;Solar-Wind-Nuclear_Subsidies&quot;</from>
					<to>Speed_of_Energy_Transition</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="34" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="241.714">
					<isee:documentation>Economic output ↑ → Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↓
When economic output grows faster (↑), the same level of investment becomes smaller relative to the expanding economy. As a result, the investment growth ratio decreases (↓).

Economic output ↓ → Investment growth relative to overall economic growth ↑
When economic output slows down (↓), even a modest level of investment can appear large relative to the weaker economy. As a result, investment growth relative to overall economic growth increases (↑) because the denominator (overall economic output) is shrinking.

</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Economic growth (GDP per capita) → Investment growth relative to overall economic growth</isee:label>
					<from>Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Investment_Growth_Relative_to_Overall_Economic_Growth</to>
				</connector>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="35" x="615.357" y="738.36" width="30" height="30">
