						<entity name="Investment"/>
						<entity name="Employment"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="clockwise" uid="39" x="457.75" y="844.25" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Climate Damage 
Balancing Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<item uid="12"/>
						<item uid="2"/>
						<entity name="Economic_Output"/>
						<entity name="Surface_Temperature_Anomaly"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="40" x="778.75" y="738.36" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Growth–Employment
–Revenue Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<item uid="15"/>
						<item uid="32"/>
						<item uid="9"/>
						<item uid="7"/>
						<entity name="Economic_Output"/>
						<entity name="Investment"/>
						<entity name="Employment"/>
						<entity name="Government_Revenue"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="41" x="941" y="738.36" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Relative Investment
–Inflation Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<item uid="34"/>
						<item uid="18"/>
						<item uid="16"/>
						<entity name="Economic_Output"/>
						<entity name="Investment_Growth_Relative_to_Overall_Economic_Growth"/>
						<entity name="Inflation"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<connector uid="42" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="89.8838">
					<from>Carbon_Tax</from>
					<to>Government_Revenue</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="44" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1.5" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="216.372">
					<isee:documentation>Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↑ → Investment ↑
When bankruptcies from stranded assets increase (↑), banks’ balance sheets weaken. To restore their asset–liability ratio and stabilize their financial position, banks often increase lending and seek new investment opportunities (↑).

Bankruptcies from stranded assets ↓ → Investment ↓
When bankruptcies decrease (↓), banks face less pressure to re-balance their portfolios. They lend more cautiously, leading to lower investment growth (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<from>Bankruptcies_Stranded_Assets</from>
					<to>Investment</to>
				</connector>
				<text_box font_weight="bold" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" uid="45" x="52.25" y="764.11" width="235" height="36.25">Reinforcing Loops → </text_box>
				<text_box font_weight="bold" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" uid="46" x="52.25" y="868.25" width="187.857" height="36.25">Balancing Loops →</text_box>
			</view>
		</views>
	</model>
	<model name="CLD_Policy_2">
		<variables>
			<aux name="Forestation" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN</eqn>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="N₂O\nEmissions" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Synthetic_Fertilizer_Production)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Surface Temperature\nAnomaly" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Carbon_Uptake_by_Forests,N₂O_Emissions,CH₄_Emissions)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Animal Product\nDemand" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Carbon Uptake\nby Forests" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Forest_Area)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Diet Shift towards\nPlant Based Foods" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk,Environmental_Consciousness)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Land Used for Food\nand Feed Production" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods,Livestock_Production)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Forest Area" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Land_Used_for_Food_and_Feed_Production,Forestation)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="CH₄ Emissions" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Livestock_Production)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Livestock\nProduction" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Animal_Product_Demand)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Perceived Climate\nChange Risk" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature_Anomaly)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Environmental\nConsciousness" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN</eqn>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Synthetic\nFertilizer\nProduction" ai_state="E">
				<eqn>NAN(Livestock_Production)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
		</variables>
		<views>
			<view isee:show_pages="false" background="#FBF5F0" page_width="1063" page_height="771" isee:page_cols="3" isee:page_rows="2" zoom="80" isee:popup_graphs_are_comparative="true" isee:enable_non_negative_highlights="false" type="stock_flow">
				<style/>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="111" x="766.969" y="757.625" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Climate-Driven Livestock
Contraction Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<entity name="Surface_Temperature_Anomaly"/>
						<entity name="Animal_Product_Demand"/>
						<entity name="Carbon_Uptake_by_Forests"/>
						<entity name="Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods"/>
						<entity name="Land_Used_for_Food_and_Feed_Production"/>
						<entity name="Forest_Area"/>
						<entity name="Livestock_Production"/>
						<entity name="Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="112" x="336.414" y="757.625" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Climate Driven 
Diet Shift Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<entity name="Surface_Temperature_Anomaly"/>
						<entity name="Animal_Product_Demand"/>
						<entity name="Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods"/>
						<entity name="CH₄_Emissions"/>
						<entity name="Livestock_Production"/>
						<entity name="Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="110" x="545.219" y="757.625" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Climate–Diet–Forest
Stabilization Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<entity name="Surface_Temperature_Anomaly"/>
						<entity name="Carbon_Uptake_by_Forests"/>
						<entity name="Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods"/>
						<entity name="Land_Used_for_Food_and_Feed_Production"/>
						<entity name="Forest_Area"/>
						<entity name="Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator color="#00007F" font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="109" x="336.414" y="851.375" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Climate Driven Diet
Shift via Fertilizer Loop</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<entity name="N₂O_Emissions"/>
						<entity name="Surface_Temperature_Anomaly"/>
						<entity name="Animal_Product_Demand"/>
						<entity name="Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods"/>
						<entity name="Livestock_Production"/>
						<entity name="Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk"/>
						<entity name="Synthetic_Fertilizer_Production"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<text_box font_weight="bold" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" uid="131" x="75" y="780.75" width="187.857" height="36.25">Balancing Loops →</text_box>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_weight="bold" font_color="black" font_size="16pt" label_side="bottom" x="185.75" y="96.434" name="Forestation">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="344.5" y="295.417" name="N₂O\nEmissions">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="371.328" y="549.98" name="Surface Temperature\nAnomaly">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="119" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="245.025">
					<isee:documentation>N₂O emissions ↑ → Surface temperature anomaly ↑
When nitrous oxide emissions increase (↑), additional greenhouse gases accumulate in the atmosphere. This strengthens radiative forcing and increases surface temperature anomaly (↑).

N₂O emissions ↓ → Surface temperature anomaly ↓
When nitrous oxide emissions decrease (↓), greenhouse forcing weakens, reducing upward pressure on surface temperature anomaly (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>N₂O emissions → Surface temperature anomaly</isee:label>
					<from>N₂O_Emissions</from>
					<to>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="794.914" y="419.667" name="Animal Product\nDemand">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="254" y="274.507" name="Carbon Uptake\nby Forests">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="805.469" y="601.98" name="Diet Shift towards\nPlant Based Foods">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="116" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="75.4681">
					<isee:documentation>Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↑ → Animal product demand ↓
When more people shift toward plant-based diets (↑), consumption of meat and other animal products declines. This reduces overall demand for animal products (↓).

Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↓ → Animal product demand ↑
When fewer people adopt plant-based diets (↓), reliance on animal-based foods increases, raising animal product demand (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Diet shift toward plant-based foods → Animal product demand</isee:label>
					<from>Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods</from>
					<to>Animal_Product_Demand</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="784.5" y="197.147" name="Land Used for Food\nand Feed Production">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="122" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="62.6724">
					<isee:documentation>Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↑ → Land Used for Food and Feed Production ↓
When diets shift toward plant-based foods (↑), demand for land-intensive livestock production declines. This reduces the total Land Used for Food and Feed Production (↓).

Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↓ → Land Used for Food and Feed Production ↑
When diets remain animal-product intensive (↓ plant shift), more land is required for grazing and feed crops, increasing Land Used for Food and Feed Production (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Diet shift toward plant-based foods → Land Used for Food and Feed Production</isee:label>
					<from>Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods</from>
					<to>Land_Used_for_Food_and_Feed_Production</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="516.551" y="96.434" name="Forest Area">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="125" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="0">
					<isee:documentation>Forestation ↑ → Forest area ↑
When forestation efforts increase (↑), more land is actively restored or planted with trees. This increases total forest area (↑).

Forestation ↓ → Forest area ↓
When forestation declines (↓), fewer new forests are established, limiting growth in forest area (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Forestation → Forest area</isee:label>
					<from>Forestation</from>
					<to>Forest_Area</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="123" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="142.877">
					<isee:documentation>Land Used for Food and Feed Production ↑ → Forest area ↓
When more land is allocated to food production (↑), forests are converted into cropland or pasture. This reduces forest area (↓).

Land Used for Food and Feed Production ↓ → Forest area ↑
When less land is required for agriculture (↓), forests can regenerate or be preserved, increasing forest area (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Land Used for Food and Feed Production → Forest area</isee:label>
					<from>Land_Used_for_Food_and_Feed_Production</from>
					<to>Forest_Area</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="113" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="190.117">
					<isee:documentation>Forest area ↑ → Carbon uptake by forests ↑
When forest area increases (↑), there are more trees and vegetation available to absorb CO₂ through photosynthesis. This increases the total amount of carbon taken up from the atmosphere, raising carbon uptake by forests (↑).

Forest area ↓ → Carbon uptake by forests ↓
When forest area decreases (↓), fewer trees are available to remove CO₂ from the atmosphere. This lowers total carbon uptake by forests (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Forest area → Carbon uptake by forests </isee:label>
					<from>Forest_Area</from>
					<to>Carbon_Uptake_by_Forests</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="454.051" y="337.147" name="CH₄ Emissions">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="129" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="223.732">
					<isee:documentation>CH₄ emissions ↑ → Surface temperature anomaly ↑
When methane emissions increase (↑), atmospheric greenhouse gas concentrations rise. Methane’s strong warming effect increases surface temperature anomaly (↑).

CH₄ emissions ↓ → Surface temperature anomaly ↓
When methane emissions decline (↓), greenhouse forcing is reduced, lowering surface temperature anomaly (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>CH₄ emissions → Surface temperature anomaly</isee:label>
					<from>CH₄_Emissions</from>
					<to>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="651.301" y="305.147" name="Livestock\nProduction">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="126" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="21.7167">
					<isee:documentation>Livestock production ↑ → Land used for food production ↑
When livestock production increases (↑), more grazing land and feed cropland are required. This expands total land used for food production (↑).

Livestock production ↓ → Land used for food production ↓
When livestock production declines (↓), less land is required for grazing and feed, reducing total land used for food production (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Livestock production → Land used for food production</isee:label>
					<from>Livestock_Production</from>
					<to>Land_Used_for_Food_and_Feed_Production</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="118" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="169.849">
					<isee:documentation>Livestock production ↑ → CH₄ emissions ↑
When livestock production increases (↑), more ruminant animals generate methane through enteric fermentation and manure management. This raises CH₄ emissions (↑).

Livestock production ↓ → CH₄ emissions ↓
When livestock production declines (↓), methane emissions from animals decrease (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Livestock production → CH₄ emissions</isee:label>
					<from>Livestock_Production</from>
					<to>CH₄_Emissions</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="117" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="124.622">
					<isee:documentation>Animal product demand ↑ → Livestock production ↑
When demand for animal products increases (↑), producers expand livestock herds and output to meet market needs. This raises livestock production (↑).

Animal product demand ↓ → Livestock production ↓
When demand declines (↓), producers reduce herd sizes and output, lowering livestock production (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Animal product demand → Livestock production</isee:label>
					<from>Animal_Product_Demand</from>
					<to>Livestock_Production</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="573.551" y="686.73" name="Perceived Climate\nChange Risk">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="121" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="6.04473">
					<isee:documentation>Perceived climate change risk ↑ → Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↑
When people perceive higher climate risk (↑), they are more likely to adjust consumption patterns toward lower-emission food choices. This strengthens the shift toward plant-based diets (↑).

Perceived climate change risk ↓ → Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↓
When perceived climate risk declines (↓), motivation for dietary change weakens, reducing the shift toward plant-based foods (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Perceived climate change risk → Diet shift toward plant-based foods </isee:label>
					<from>Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk</from>
					<to>Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="120" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="298.051">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature anomaly ↑ → Perceived climate change risk ↑
When surface temperatures rise (↑), climate impacts such as heatwaves, droughts, and extreme events become more visible. This increases public perception of climate change risk (↑).

Surface temperature anomaly ↓ → Perceived climate change risk ↓
When temperature anomalies stabilize or decline (↓), perceived urgency and climate risk awareness tend to decrease (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface temperature anomaly → Perceived climate change risk</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</from>
					<to>Perceived_Climate_Change_Risk</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_weight="bold" font_color="black" font_size="16pt" label_side="bottom" x="1007.72" y="601.98" name="Environmental\nConsciousness">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="124" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside_stem" angle="180">
					<isee:documentation>Environmental consciousness ↑ → Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↑
When environmental awareness increases (↑), individuals are more likely to adopt sustainable consumption patterns. This strengthens the shift toward plant-based diets (↑).

Environmental consciousness ↓ → Diet shift toward plant-based foods ↓
When environmental awareness declines (↓), motivation for dietary sustainability weakens, reducing the plant-based shift (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Environmental consciousness → Diet shift toward plant-based foods </isee:label>
					<from>Environmental_Consciousness</from>
					<to>Diet_Shift_towards_Plant_Based_Foods</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#001F3E" font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="523.469" y="202.147" name="Synthetic\nFertilizer\nProduction">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="128" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="188.519">
					<isee:documentation>Synthetic fertilizer production ↑ → N₂O emissions ↑
When synthetic fertilizer production and application increase (↑), more reactive nitrogen is introduced into agricultural soils. Microbial processes convert excess nitrogen into nitrous oxide, increasing N₂O emissions (↑).

Synthetic fertilizer production ↓ → N₂O emissions ↓
When synthetic fertilizer production declines (↓), less reactive nitrogen is applied to soils, reducing N₂O emissions (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Man-made fertilizer production → N₂O emissions</isee:label>
					<from>Synthetic_Fertilizer_Production</from>
					<to>N₂O_Emissions</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="127" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="109.857">
					<isee:documentation>Livestock production ↑ → Synthetic fertilizer production ↓
When livestock production increases (↑), more nutrients become available through manure from livestock systems. This reduces the reliance on synthetic fertilizers for crop production. As a result, synthetic fertilizer production decreases (↓).

Livestock production ↓ → Synthetic fertilizer production ↑
When livestock production declines (↓), less manure is available as a nutrient source. Crop production becomes more dependent on synthetic fertilizers to maintain yields. As a result, synthetic fertilizer production increases (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Livestock production → Man-made fertilizer production</isee:label>
					<from>Livestock_Production</from>
					<to>Synthetic_Fertilizer_Production</to>
				</connector>
				<text_box font_weight="bold" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" uid="132" x="73.25" y="873.86" width="235" height="36.25">Reinforcing Loops → </text_box>
				<connector uid="114" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" isee:thickness="1" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_stem" angle="253.839">
					<isee:documentation>Carbon uptake by forests ↑ → Surface temperature anomaly ↓
When forests absorb more carbon (↑), more CO₂ is removed from the atmosphere. This weakens the greenhouse effect and reduces the surface temperature anomaly (↓).

Carbon uptake by forests ↓ → Surface temperature anomaly ↑
When forests absorb less carbon (↓), more CO₂ remains in the atmosphere. This strengthens the greenhouse effect and increases the surface temperature anomaly (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Carbon uptake by forests → Surface temperature anomaly </isee:label>
					<from>Carbon_Uptake_by_Forests</from>
					<to>Surface_Temperature_Anomaly</to>
				</connector>
			</view>
		</views>
	</model>
	<model name="CLD_Policy_3">
		<variables>
			<aux name="Speed of Implementation\nEnergy Tax or Subsidy" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN</eqn>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Economic Output" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Energy_Investment,Inflation,Stranded_Fossil_Energy_Assets)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Energy Production\nTransformation" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Economic_Output,Big_Changes_in_Energy_Costs)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Stranded Fossil\nEnergy Assets" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Energy_Production_Transformation)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Energy\nInvestment" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Energy_Production_Transformation)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Transition\nMalinvestment" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Energy_Production_Transformation)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Inflation" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Transition_Malinvestment)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Big Changes in\nEnergy Costs" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Speed_of_Implementation_Energy_Tax_or_Subsidy)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
		</variables>
		<views>
			<view isee:show_pages="false" background="#FBF5F0" page_width="1063" page_height="771" isee:page_cols="2" isee:page_rows="2" zoom="80" isee:popup_graphs_are_comparative="true" isee:enable_non_negative_highlights="false" type="stock_flow">
				<style/>
				<aux font_weight="bold" font_color="black" font_size="16pt" x="879.024" y="704.117" name="Speed of Implementation\nEnergy Tax or Subsidy">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="433.664" y="516.412" name="Economic Output">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="708.274" y="516.412" name="Energy Production\nTransformation">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="580.358" y="108.097" name="Stranded Fossil\nEnergy Assets">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="1" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="130.908">
					<isee:documentation>Speed of Implementation ↑ → Big Changes in Energy Costs ↑
When policies are implemented more quickly (↑), energy prices or incentives change more sharply over a short period. This creates large changes in energy costs (↑).

Speed of Implementation ↓ → Big Changes in Energy Costs ↓
When policies are implemented more gradually (↓), changes in energy prices are spread over time. This results in smaller and more manageable changes in energy costs (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Speed of Implementation → Big Changes in Energy Costs (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Speed_of_Implementation_Energy_Tax_or_Subsidy</from>
					<to>Big_Changes_in_Energy_Costs</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="580.358" y="627.85" name="Energy\nInvestment">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="2" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="248.443">
					<isee:documentation>Energy Production Transformation ↑ → Energy Investment ↑
As the energy system transforms more rapidly (↑), more investment is required in new infrastructure, technologies, and supply chains. This increases energy investment (↑).

Energy Production Transformation ↓ → Energy Investment ↓
When transformation slows down (↓), fewer changes are required in infrastructure and technology. This reduces the need for new investment (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Energy Production Transformation → Energy Investment (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Energy_Production_Transformation</from>
					<to>Energy_Investment</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="3" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="171.181">
					<isee:documentation>Energy Investment ↑ → Economic Output ↑
Higher investment in energy systems (↑) increases economic activity through production, construction, and employment. This raises economic output (↑).

Energy Investment ↓ → Economic Output ↓
Lower levels of investment (↓) reduce economic activity and slow production. This decreases economic output (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Energy Investment → Economic Output (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Energy_Investment</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="4" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="80.0985">
					<isee:documentation>Economic Output ↑ → Energy Production Transformation ↑
When economic output increases (↑), more resources and capital are available to support transformation. This enables faster changes in the energy system (↑).

Economic Output ↓ → Energy Production Transformation ↓
When economic output declines (↓), fewer resources are available for transformation. This slows changes in the energy system (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Economic Output → Energy Production Transformation (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Energy_Production_Transformation</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="670.858" y="245.533" name="Transition\nMalinvestment">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="5" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="65.0419">
					<isee:documentation>Energy Production Transformation ↑ → Transition Malinvestment ↑
When transformation happens rapidly (↑), investments are made under time pressure and uncertainty. This increases the likelihood of inefficient or poorly planned investments (↑).

Energy Production Transformation ↓ → Transition Malinvestment ↓
When transformation occurs more gradually (↓), investments can be planned and coordinated more effectively. This reduces inefficient investments (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Energy Production Transformation → Transition Malinvestment (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Energy_Production_Transformation</from>
					<to>Transition_Malinvestment</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="461.857" y="249.283" name="Inflation">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="6" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="153.404">
					<isee:documentation>Transition Malinvestment ↑ → Inflation ↑
Higher levels of inefficient investment (↑) increase costs across the system, including wasted resources and misallocated capital. These cost pressures contribute to higher inflation (↑).

Transition Malinvestment ↓ → Inflation ↓
Lower levels of inefficient investment (↓) reduce cost pressures and improve efficiency. This helps keep inflation lower (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Transition Malinvestment → Inflation (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Transition_Malinvestment</from>
					<to>Inflation</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="7" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="237.307">
					<isee:documentation>Inflation ↑ → Economic Output ↓
When inflation rises (↑), production costs increase and purchasing power declines. This reduces economic efficiency and lowers economic output (↓).

Inflation ↓ → Economic Output ↑
When inflation decreases (↓), costs stabilize and economic efficiency improves. This supports higher economic output (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Inflation → Economic Output (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Inflation</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<isee:loop_indicator font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="clockwise" uid="8" x="358.414" y="744.617" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Energy Transition 
Growth Engine</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<item uid="2"/>
						<item uid="3"/>
						<item uid="4"/>
						<entity name="Energy_Production_Transformation"/>
						<entity name="Energy_Investment"/>
						<entity name="Economic_Output"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<isee:loop_indicator font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="9" x="358.414" y="840.125" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Transition-Induced
Inflation Brake</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<item uid="5"/>
						<item uid="6"/>
						<item uid="7"/>
						<item uid="4"/>
						<entity name="Energy_Production_Transformation"/>
						<entity name="Transition_Malinvestment"/>
						<entity name="Inflation"/>
						<entity name="Economic_Output"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<connector uid="10" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="40.8128">
					<isee:documentation>Energy Production Transformation ↑ → Stranded Fossil Energy Assets ↑
When the energy system transforms rapidly (↑), existing fossil fuel infrastructure and investments lose value before their expected lifetime. This increases stranded fossil energy assets (↑).

Energy Production Transformation ↓ → Stranded Fossil Energy Assets ↓
When transformation is slower (↓), existing assets have more time to adapt or depreciate gradually. This reduces the creation of stranded fossil energy assets (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Energy Production Transformation → Stranded Fossil Energy Assets (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Energy_Production_Transformation</from>
					<to>Stranded_Fossil_Energy_Assets</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="11" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="184.174">
					<isee:documentation>Stranded Fossil Energy Assets ↑ → Economic Output ↓
An increase in stranded fossil energy assets (↑) leads to financial losses, reduced investment returns, and economic disruption. This lowers economic output (↓).

Stranded Fossil Energy Assets ↓ → Economic Output ↑
When fewer assets are stranded (↓), financial losses are reduced and economic activity remains more stable. This supports higher economic output (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Stranded Fossil Energy Assets → Economic Output (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Stranded_Fossil_Energy_Assets</from>
					<to>Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<isee:loop_indicator font_size="14pt" text="" orientation="counter_clockwise" uid="12" x="505.149" y="840.125" width="30" height="30">
					<name>Stranded Asset
Economic Drag</name>
					<isee:entities>
						<item uid="10"/>
						<item uid="11"/>
						<item uid="4"/>
						<entity name="Energy_Production_Transformation"/>
						<entity name="Stranded_Fossil_Energy_Assets"/>
						<entity name="Economic_Output"/>
					</isee:entities>
				</isee:loop_indicator>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="16pt" x="808.608" y="622.85" name="Big Changes in\nEnergy Costs">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="13" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="133.309">
					<isee:documentation>Big Changes in Energy Costs ↑ → Energy Production Transformation ↑
When energy costs change significantly (↑), firms are strongly incentivized to shift technologies and production methods. This accelerates transformation of the energy system (↑).

Big Changes in Energy Costs ↓ → Energy Production Transformation ↓
When changes in energy costs are small (↓), there is less pressure to adjust production systems. Transformation of the energy system slows down (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Big Changes in Energy Costs → Energy Production Transformation (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Big_Changes_in_Energy_Costs</from>
					<to>Energy_Production_Transformation</to>
				</connector>
				<text_box font_weight="bold" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" uid="19" x="120" y="860.75" width="167.5" height="36.25">Balancing Loops →</text_box>
				<text_box font_weight="bold" font_size="16pt" appearance="transparent" uid="20" x="120" y="768.61" width="167.5" height="36.25">Reinforcing Loops → </text_box>
			</view>
		</views>
	</model>
	<model name="CLD_Policy_4">
		<variables>
			<aux name="Radiative\nForcing" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Emissions,Methane_Lifetime,Ozone_Lifetime,Atmospheric_CO₂)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Surface\nTemperature" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Radiative_Forcing)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Sea Level Rise" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Climate Impacts" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature,Sea_Level_Rise)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Emissions" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Climate_Impacts)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Methane Lifetime" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Ozone Lifetime" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Land Carbon Uptake" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Atmospheric_CO₂,Vegetation_Damage,Vegetation_Growth)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Atmospheric CO₂" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Emissions,Land_Carbon_Uptake,Ocean_Carbon_Uptake)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Ocean\nCarbon Uptake" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Atmospheric_CO₂)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Vegetation Damage" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Vegetation Growth" ai_state="F">
				<eqn>NAN(Surface_Temperature)</eqn>
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units>Dimensionless</units>
			</aux>
		</variables>
		<views>
			<view isee:show_pages="false" background="#FBF5F0" page_width="1063" page_height="771" isee:page_cols="2" isee:page_rows="2" zoom="80" isee:popup_graphs_are_comparative="true" isee:enable_non_negative_highlights="false" type="stock_flow">
				<style/>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="557.417" y="345.333" name="Radiative\nForcing">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="1010.88" y="397.333" name="Surface\nTemperature">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="2" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="22.7328">
					<isee:documentation>Radiative forcing ↑ → Surface temperature ↑
When radiative forcing increases (↑), more energy is trapped in the Earth’s system due to greenhouse gases. This leads to a rise in surface temperature (↑).

Radiative forcing ↓ → Surface temperature ↓
When radiative forcing decreases (↓), less heat is retained in the atmosphere. This reduces surface temperature (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Radiative_Forcing → Surface_Temperature (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Radiative_Forcing</from>
					<to>Surface_Temperature</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="885.75" y="621.667" name="Sea Level Rise">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="574.917" y="636.167" name="Climate Impacts">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="3" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="241.291">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature ↑ → Climate impacts ↑
When surface temperature increases (↑), the frequency and intensity of climate-related impacts such as extreme weather, droughts, and heatwaves rise. This increases overall climate impacts (↑).

Surface temperature ↓ → Climate impacts ↓
When surface temperature decreases (↓), climate-related disruptions become less severe. This reduces climate impacts (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface_Temperature → Climate_Impacts (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature</from>
					<to>Climate_Impacts</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="4" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="263.516">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature ↑ → Sea level rise ↑
When surface temperature increases (↑), thermal expansion of seawater and melting of glaciers and ice sheets accelerate. This increases sea level rise (↑).

Surface temperature ↓ → Sea level rise ↓
When surface temperature decreases (↓), the rate of ice melt and thermal expansion slows. This reduces sea level rise (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface_Temperature → Sea_Level_Rise (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature</from>
					<to>Sea_Level_Rise</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="373.875" y="568.667" name="Emissions">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="5" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="173.639">
					<isee:documentation>Climate impacts ↑ → Emissions ↓
When climate impacts increase (↑), disruptions to economic activity, infrastructure, and energy systems can reduce production and consumption. This leads to lower emissions (↓).

Climate impacts ↓ → Emissions ↑
When climate impacts decrease (↓), economic activity and energy use can expand. This leads to higher emissions (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Climate_Impacts → Emissions (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Climate_Impacts</from>
					<to>Emissions</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="6" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="74.3157">
					<isee:documentation>Emissions ↑ → Radiative forcing ↑
When emissions increase (↑), more greenhouse gases accumulate in the atmosphere. This strengthens radiative forcing (↑).

Emissions ↓ → Radiative forcing ↓
When emissions decrease (↓), fewer greenhouse gases are added to the atmosphere. This weakens radiative forcing (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Emissions → Radiative_Forcing (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Emissions</from>
					<to>Radiative_Forcing</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="7" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="200.923">
					<isee:documentation>Sea level rise ↑ → Climate impacts ↑
When sea level rise increases (↑), coastal flooding, erosion, and infrastructure damage become more severe. This increases climate impacts (↑).

Sea level rise ↓ → Climate impacts ↓
When sea level rise decreases (↓), coastal risks are reduced. This lowers climate impacts (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Sea_Level_Rise → Climate_Impacts (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Sea_Level_Rise</from>
					<to>Climate_Impacts</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="749.25" y="438.083" name="Methane Lifetime">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="8" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="209.083">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature ↑ → Methane lifetime ↓
When surface temperature increases (↑), atmospheric chemical reactions accelerate, reducing the lifetime of methane in the atmosphere (↓).

Surface temperature ↓ → Methane lifetime ↑
When surface temperature decreases (↓), atmospheric reaction rates slow down, increasing methane lifetime (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface_Temperature → Methane_Lifetime (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature</from>
					<to>Methane_Lifetime</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="9" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="169.414">
					<isee:documentation>Methane lifetime ↑ → Radiative forcing ↑
When methane lifetime increases (↑), methane remains in the atmosphere longer, increasing its contribution to radiative forcing (↑).

Methane lifetime ↓ → Radiative forcing ↓
When methane lifetime decreases (↓), methane is removed more quickly from the atmosphere, reducing radiative forcing (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Methane_Lifetime → Radiative_Forcing (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Methane_Lifetime</from>
					<to>Radiative_Forcing</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="661.25" y="525.417" name="Ozone Lifetime">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="10" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="222.393">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature ↑ → Ozone lifetime ↓
When surface temperature increases (↑), atmospheric reactions that break down ozone accelerate, reducing ozone lifetime (↓).

Surface temperature ↓ → Ozone lifetime ↑
When surface temperature decreases (↓), ozone persists longer in the atmosphere, increasing its lifetime (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface_Temperature → Ozone_Lifetime (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature</from>
					<to>Ozone_Lifetime</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="11" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="148.203">
					<isee:documentation>Ozone lifetime ↑ → Radiative forcing ↑
When ozone lifetime increases (↑), ozone remains in the atmosphere longer, enhancing radiative forcing (↑).

Ozone lifetime ↓ → Radiative forcing ↓
When ozone lifetime decreases (↓), ozone is removed more quickly, reducing radiative forcing (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Ozone_Lifetime → Radiative_Forcing (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Ozone_Lifetime</from>
					<to>Radiative_Forcing</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="390.375" y="163.167" name="Land Carbon Uptake">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="258.375" y="382.333" name="Atmospheric CO₂">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="12" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="21.6276">
					<isee:documentation>Atmospheric CO₂ ↑ → Radiative forcing ↑
When atmospheric CO₂ increases (↑), more heat is trapped in the Earth’s system due to the greenhouse effect. This increases radiative forcing (↑).

Atmospheric CO₂ ↓ → Radiative forcing ↓
When atmospheric CO₂ decreases (↓), less heat is retained in the atmosphere. This reduces radiative forcing (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Atmospheric_CO₂ → Radiative_Forcing (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Atmospheric_CO₂</from>
					<to>Radiative_Forcing</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="13" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="137.214">
					<isee:documentation>Emissions ↑ → Atmospheric CO₂ ↑
When emissions increase (↑), more CO₂ is released into the atmosphere. This raises atmospheric CO₂ levels (↑).

Emissions ↓ → Atmospheric CO₂ ↓
When emissions decrease (↓), less CO₂ is added to the atmosphere. This lowers atmospheric CO₂ levels (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Emissions → Atmospheric_CO₂ (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Emissions</from>
					<to>Atmospheric_CO₂</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="14" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="261.741">
					<isee:documentation>Land carbon uptake ↑ → Atmospheric CO₂ ↓
When land carbon uptake increases (↑), more CO₂ is absorbed by vegetation and soils. This reduces atmospheric CO₂ (↓).

Land carbon uptake ↓ → Atmospheric CO₂ ↑
When land carbon uptake decreases (↓), less CO₂ is removed from the atmosphere. This increases atmospheric CO₂ (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Land_Carbon_Uptake → Atmospheric_CO₂ (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Land_Carbon_Uptake</from>
					<to>Atmospheric_CO₂</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="90" y="382.333" name="Ocean\nCarbon Uptake">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="15" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="38.9902">
					<isee:documentation>Ocean carbon uptake ↑ → Atmospheric CO₂ ↓
When ocean carbon uptake increases (↑), more CO₂ is absorbed by the oceans. This reduces atmospheric CO₂ (↓).

Ocean carbon uptake ↓ → Atmospheric CO₂ ↑
When ocean carbon uptake decreases (↓), less CO₂ is absorbed. This increases atmospheric CO₂ (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Ocean_Carbon_Uptake → Atmospheric_CO₂ (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Ocean_Carbon_Uptake</from>
					<to>Atmospheric_CO₂</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="16" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="94.3896">
					<isee:documentation>Atmospheric CO₂ ↑ → Land carbon uptake ↑
When atmospheric CO₂ increases (↑), plants can photosynthesize more efficiently under certain conditions. This increases land carbon uptake (↑).

Atmospheric CO₂ ↓ → Land carbon uptake ↓
When atmospheric CO₂ decreases (↓), this fertilization effect weakens. This reduces land carbon uptake (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Atmospheric_CO₂ → Land_Carbon_Uptake (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Atmospheric_CO₂</from>
					<to>Land_Carbon_Uptake</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="17" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="221.814">
					<isee:documentation>Atmospheric CO₂ ↑ → Ocean carbon uptake ↑
When atmospheric CO₂ increases (↑), more CO₂ dissolves into ocean water. This increases ocean carbon uptake (↑).

Atmospheric CO₂ ↓ → Ocean carbon uptake ↓
When atmospheric CO₂ decreases (↓), less CO₂ is available to dissolve. This reduces ocean carbon uptake (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Atmospheric_CO₂ → Ocean_Carbon_Uptake (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Atmospheric_CO₂</from>
					<to>Ocean_Carbon_Uptake</to>
				</connector>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="609" y="230.073" name="Vegetation Damage">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux font_color="black" font_size="14pt" x="777.75" y="143.167" name="Vegetation Growth">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="18" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="122.74">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature ↑ → Vegetation damage ↑
When surface temperature increases (↑), heat stress, drought, and extreme events damage ecosystems. This increases vegetation damage (↑).

Surface temperature ↓ → Vegetation damage ↓
When surface temperature decreases (↓), stress on ecosystems is reduced. This lowers vegetation damage (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface_Temperature → Vegetation_Damage (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature</from>
					<to>Vegetation_Damage</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="19" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="135.672">
					<isee:documentation>Vegetation damage ↑ → Land carbon uptake ↓
When vegetation damage increases (↑), plant health and biomass decline. This reduces the ability of ecosystems to absorb carbon (↓).

Vegetation damage ↓ → Land carbon uptake ↑
When vegetation damage decreases (↓), ecosystems recover and absorb more carbon. This increases land carbon uptake (↑).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Vegetation_Damage → Land_Carbon_Uptake (−)</isee:label>
					<from>Vegetation_Damage</from>
					<to>Land_Carbon_Uptake</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="20" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="inside" angle="103.996">
					<isee:documentation>Surface temperature ↑ → Vegetation growth ↑
When surface temperature increases (↑), growing seasons may lengthen and plant growth can increase under suitable conditions. This increases vegetation growth (↑).

Surface temperature ↓ → Vegetation growth ↓
When surface temperature decreases (↓), growing conditions may become less favorable. This reduces vegetation growth (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Surface_Temperature → Vegetation_Growth (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Surface_Temperature</from>
					<to>Vegetation_Growth</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="21" color="#00007F" font_color="#00007F" font_size="20pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside" angle="157.175">
					<isee:documentation>Vegetation growth ↑ → Land carbon uptake ↑
When vegetation growth increases (↑), more biomass is produced, enhancing carbon absorption. This increases land carbon uptake (↑).

Vegetation growth ↓ → Land carbon uptake ↓
When vegetation growth decreases (↓), less carbon is absorbed. This reduces land carbon uptake (↓).</isee:documentation>
					<isee:label>Vegetation_Growth → Land_Carbon_Uptake (+)</isee:label>
					<from>Vegetation_Growth</from>
					<to>Land_Carbon_Uptake</to>
				</connector>
				<isee:loop_indicator text="" orientation="clockwise" uid="22" x="240" y="718" width="30" height="30">
